<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oka crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1990</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kanesatake resistance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oka crisis was a 78-day armed stand-off in 1990 between Mohawk protesters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> police and Canadian army near the town of Oka, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. It began when Mohawks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesatake">Kanesatake</a> blocked a road to stop a golf course from being extended onto disputed land, including a burial ground, and a police officer was killed in a raid. It drew national attention to indigenous land claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oka-crisis.jpg" alt="Oka crisis" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SamuelFreli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crise_d%27Oka_-_SQ_et_forces_canadiennes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
